<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murong Nuohebo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 688</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> Nuohebo was the last ruler of Tuyuhun, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> kingdom around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Qinghai Lake</a>, reigning as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> and married to a Tang princess. When the Tibetan Empire conquered Tuyuhun in 663 he fled with his followers into Tang territory. The Tang defeat at the Dafei River in 670 ended attempts to restore him, and his people were resettled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The year of Murong Nuohebo's birth is not known. In 635 his grandfather, the Busabo Khan Murong Fuyun, whose border raids had provoked the Tang, was killed in flight from an army under the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jing">Li Jing</a>. His father Murong Shun surrendered and was made khan, but he had spent years at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> court, his mother was the Sui princess Guanghua, and the Tuyuhun thought him too Chinese; late in 635 he was assassinated. Nuohebo, said to be young and holding the title Prince of Yan, succeeded, and Emperor Taizong sent the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-junji">Hou Junji</a> to help him pacify his people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 636 Nuohebo submitted to the Tang as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and sent a younger brother as a hostage, and Taizong made him Prince of Heyuan and Ledou Khan. From 638 the Tibetan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>, who believed Nuohebo had blocked his request for a Tang bride, attacked Tuyuhun and drove its people north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Qinghai Lake</a>. In 639 Nuohebo visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and married Princess Honghua. By 641 his chief minister, the Prince of Xuan, plotted to seize them both and go over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>; Nuohebo fled to the general the Prince of Weixin at Shanshan, and the Tang officer Xi Junmai killed the plotter.</p><p>Tibetan attacks resumed in 660 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gar-trinring-tsendro">Gar Trinring Tsendro</a>, son of the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gar-tongtsen">Gar Tongtsen</a>. In 663 the official Suhe Gui fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> with Tuyuhun secrets, Tibetan forces defeated the Tuyuhun, and Nuohebo and Princess Honghua led their people to Tang's Liang Prefecture, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Emperor Gaozong made Nuohebo Prince of Qinghai around 666, perhaps to show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> that he still meant to restore Tuyuhun, but declined to fight for it until 670, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xue-rengui">Xue Rengui</a>'s army was routed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dafei-river">Battle of Dafeichuan</a>. In 672 the Tuyuhun were settled at Shanzhou and soon moved to Lingzhou, where a prefecture, Anlezhou, was made for them with Nuohebo as prefect. He kept the title of khan until his death in 688. His son Murong Zhong inherited his titles, which passed down at least four more generations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murong Nuohebo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
